<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The send mark, and the files page's "up one level": Lucide's `arrow-up`.

    ## Why an arrow rather than a paper plane

    The composer's send control used to carry the library's own `Send`, which is a paper
    plane — a *postal* figure, drawn at the weight of an illustration rather than of a
    control. The chat apps this page is read beside (ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini) all send
    with an arrow, and the arrow is also the only figure that has no second meaning on
    a phone: a plane says "share" in most Android surfaces, while an arrow up beside a
    text field says "send what I typed" everywhere.

    The same glyph is drawn a second time on the files page, where it means "open the
    parent directory". One vector, two call sites, and the call site decides the size —
    the rule for a borrowed glyph (see ARCHITECTURE §12.3).

    ## Provenance

    Lucide's own single-stroke figure, on its 24-unit grid, stroke 2 of 24 with round
    caps — the same weight `ic_check.xml` and `ic_copy.xml` are drawn at, so the three
    marks are one family:

        M12 19V5 m5 12 7-7 7 7

    Source: https://github.com/lucide-icons/lucide (ISC).
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">

    <path
        android:pathData="M12 19 L12 5 M5 12 L12 5 L19 12"
        android:strokeColor="#FF000000"
        android:strokeWidth="2"
        android:strokeLineCap="round"
        android:strokeLineJoin="round"
        android:fillColor="#00000000" />
</vector>
